//this pointer
this is a pointer that points to the current object.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int age;

    void setAge(int age) {
        this->age = age;
    }
};

Suppose:

Student s1;
s1.setAge(20);

Inside setAge():

age → function parameter
this->age → s1's data member

So:

this->age = age;

means:

Set the current object's age to the given age.

Why do we need this?

Mainly when a data member and parameter have the same name.

Without this:

void setAge(int age) {
    age = age;  // parameter assigned to itself ❌
}

With this:

void setAge(int age) {
    this->age = age;  // ✅
}

Simple definition:

this is a pointer that refers to the current object inside a member function.